README-METAFONT -- rendering a font on this disk================================================The TeX tree ships thirty programs, and eight of them are about BITMAPfonts: virmf, inimf, gftype, gftopk, pktype, pktogf, vptovf and vftovp.Until the base was built there was nothing on this disk for any of themto read, and this file is what that took.Why there are no .pk files here-------------------------------There are none, deliberately, and there never will be many.  A .pk is abitmap rendered for ONE printer at ONE resolution.  A collection cannotcarry the set; what it carries is Metafont, which makes the one you want.That is also what TeX's own MakeTeXPK does -- CMDS/TEXCMDS/maketexpk ishere -- and it is why SYS/TEX/FONTS holds .tfm METRICS and no glyphs.So the DVI drivers all warn that a font file could not be opened, and setthe text at zero size.  That is the collection being honest about what ithas, not a fault in the drivers.The base, which now ships-------------------------Metafont needs a preloaded base the way TeX needs a format.  SYS/TEX/FORMATShas shipped plain.fmt, lplain.fmt and splain.fmt since the tree landed;SYS/TEX/MFBASES held only its Makefile and install.script, so virmf stoppedat    I can't find the default base file!`inimf' builds it out of SYS/TEX/MFINPUTS, in about a minute:    ksh -c "cd /dd/tmp; inimf < mf.in"where mf.in is the one line install.script asks for:    plain; \input modes; dumpThat writes plain.base, 80144 bytes.  It is now in SYS/TEX/MFBASES andvirmf finds it with nothing set.  Build your own the same way if you wanta base with local modes in it.Rendering a font----------------logo10.mf is the smallest interesting font here -- the seven letters of theMETAFONT logo, and it needs no Computer Modern.  Pick a mode out ofMFINPUTS/modes.mf; `epsonlo' is a low-resolution one, which keeps thebitmap small.    echo "\\mode=epsonlo; mode_setup; input logo10" > /dd/tmp/run.mf    ksh -c "cd /dd/tmp; virmf < run.mf"Metafont answers with the character codes as it renders them and writesthree files: logo10.120gf (the bitmap), logo10.tfm (the metrics TeX reads)and logo10.log.`ksh -c "cd ..."' rather than plain `cd', because Metafont names its ownoutput files and writes them into the DATA directory -- and bash's `cd'does not move that.  See README-SHELLS.THE SEARCH PATHS DO NOT INCLUDE THE CURRENT DIRECTORY-----------------------------------------------------This is the one thing here that reads like a fault and is not.  The pathsare compiled into the binaries, and the font ones are    /dd/SYS/TEX/FONTS:/dd/USR/TEX/FONTSwith no leading `.', where the Metafont SOURCE path does begin with one.So gftype, gftopk, pktype, pktogf, vftovp and vptovf will tell you    logo10.120gf: GF file not foundabout a file that is in front of you.  Name the directory in the matchingenvironment variable and they find it:    export GFFONTS=/dd/tmp    export PKFONTS=/dd/tmp    export VFFONTS=/dd/tmp    export TEXFONTS=/dd/tmp:/dd/SYS/TEX/FONTS/TFMThe full set the binaries read is GFFONTS, PKFONTS, VFFONTS, TEXFONTS,TEXINPUTS, TEXFORMATS, TEXPOOL, MFINPUTS, MFBASES and MFPOOL.  An OUTPUTfilename is never searched for, so an absolute output path works with novariable set at all.Reading what Metafont wrote---------------------------    gftype -i logo10.120gfprints the glyphs as asterisks, which is the only way to SEE a character ona terminal.  `-m' adds the mnemonic opcodes beside them.    gftopk logo10.120gf logo10.120pkpacks it -- 608 bytes down to 364 for this font.  `pktype' prints the packedform back, glyphs included, and `pktogf' unpacks it.  The unpacked filecomes back 632 bytes rather than 608: pktogf rewrites the preamble commentas `GFtoPK 2.3 output from METAFONT output <date>' where Metafont wrote`METAFONT output <date>'.  The bitmap is unchanged; the header grew.    tftopl logo10.tfm logo10.plreads the metrics -- the file TeX itself would use, where the .gf is onlyfor the printer.Virtual fonts-------------A virtual font has no glyphs at all.  Each character is a short programsaying what to set from some REAL font, so one .vf can gather charactersfrom several fonts, shift them, or draw rules between them.    vptovf /dd/DOC/tex/sample.vpl /dd/tmp/s.vf /dd/tmp/s.tfm    vftovp s.vf s.tfm /dd/tmp/back.vplDOC/tex/sample.vpl is a one-character font written for this: it maps `A'onto cmr10's `A' and stops.  vftovp reads the binary pair back to the textit came from, and fills in cmr10's real checksum on the way, which thehand-written source left at zero.